WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:04.938
-Our first guest tonight
is a Grammy Award winning actor

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:07.540
currently starring
in "Gutenberg! The Musical!,"

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.209
which is on Broadway now
at the James Earl Jones Theater.

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:12.378
Please welcome to the show
for the very first time,

00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:13.880
Josh Gad, everybody.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.048
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:30.997 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:33.199
Welcome to the show.

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:37.103
-Fred, I so want to go
to a 1000s party now.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:40.539
I really I have to go
to an airport this week and go.

00:00:40.539 --> 00:00:41.641
-I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:42.909
And I can't believe

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:43.943
you've never been here.
-I'm so happy that I --

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:46.413
No, but that's your fault.
-Yeah.

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:48.080
I can't invite myself
on your show.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.449
I've tried.
-Yeah.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:52.151
-But it doesn't work that way,
from what I understand.

00:00:52.151 --> 00:00:54.320
-Well, I'm very glad we've
rectified the situation.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.190
And I hope this is a situation
where you come back and back.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.658
-I will be here.

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:01.660
-By the way, I'm so grateful
for the fact that you were here

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:03.562
on a night where
you're doing a show on Broadway.

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:04.830
-I'm going to -- I'm going
to leave here

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.199
and I'm going to dance
for another audience.

00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:07.200
-Yeah.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.335
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:12.705
But for now,
I speak to you.

00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:14.573
-Does this serve in any way,

00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:16.175
shape or form
as a warmup for you?

00:01:16.175 --> 00:01:17.610
-This is my warmup.
-Okay.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.479
-Do you guys want to see
what I do for a warmup?

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:20.480
-Yes.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.647
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:22.848
-It's very --

00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:26.085
So I start off
by doing stretches like this.

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:27.120
-Okay.
Okay. Gotcha.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.655
Which, by the way,
I'm pretty limber

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:30.623
for a guy my size.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:31.624
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:32.758
So I do this.
-Okay.

00:01:32.758 --> 00:01:33.859
-And then I do lip trills.

00:01:33.859 --> 00:01:34.927
You guys know
what lip trills are?

00:01:34.927 --> 00:01:36.863
Brrr.
-Okay.

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:39.331
-Lip trills. None of you
should do that.

00:01:39.331 --> 00:01:41.801
And then -- And then
I sing '80s pop ballads.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.135
-You do?
-I do.

00:01:43.135 --> 00:01:44.603
-And have you found
historically,

00:01:44.603 --> 00:01:47.172
through a life on Broadway,
that that's helpful for you?

00:01:47.172 --> 00:01:50.476
-There's so much falsetto
in '80s songs for some reason.

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:51.777
So go high.

00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:54.647
I do "Take on Me" by A-ha,
I do some others.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:58.117
And Andrew Rannells, my costar,
brilliant costar, by the way.

00:01:58.117 --> 00:01:59.118
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:01.487
Thank you.
He's -- He's not here.

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:03.556
-He's not here. Let's not.
-You don't need a cheer for him.

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:06.292
He -- But -- But he does
Sondheim songs.

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:09.428
So this is very complicated.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:09.428 --> 00:02:12.198
-Just infusion of different
sounds backstage

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:13.466
when you come visit
our dressing room.

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:16.102
-And you have
adjacent dressing rooms.

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:18.037
-Yeah. Unfortunately
for Andrew, that is accurate.

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:21.107
We have --
We're separated by a bathroom.

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:22.641
A lot of bad things
happen in that bathroom

00:02:22.641 --> 00:02:24.944
before I go on stage,
but we have a bathroom,

00:02:24.944 --> 00:02:26.612
and then our two
dressing rooms.

00:02:26.612 --> 00:02:28.181
-Are the walls thin enough

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:29.915
that you don't hear
each other or you do?

00:02:29.915 --> 00:02:32.084
-No, they're frighteningly thin?
-Yeah.

00:02:32.084 --> 00:02:34.253
-They're frighteningly thin,
uncomfortably thin walls.

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:37.389
-Everything about Broadway
dressing rooms is frightening

00:02:37.389 --> 00:02:38.991
compared to what
your expectations are.

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:42.128
They are.
-If you love --

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:44.897
If you love,
like, getting sinus infections,

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:46.065
it's a great place
to hang out.

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:47.667
-Yeah.
-There's a lot --

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:50.102
There's a lot of dust
mites in there, which is great.

00:02:50.102 --> 00:02:51.437
-Yeah. You know,
when I've been lucky enough

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:53.906
to go backstage to say hi
to friends and shows, like,

00:02:53.906 --> 00:02:55.474
it's so cramped.
-It's very cramped.

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:57.810
You feel bad visiting.
It's like, if --

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.413
-You're making me feel bad for
having to go there after this,

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:01.614
actually, like,
now I'm starting...

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:03.816
-It's like going to visit
a friend in an elevator.

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.351
-Yeah, yeah.
-It's before they go up.

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:07.186
-That is 100 --
Except you go nowhere.

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:08.921
It doesn't even go up and down.
-Yeah.

00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:11.958
-You just sit and have to listen
to people going to the bathroom.

00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:13.926
-Now, it is crazy.

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:17.030
I mean, you and Andrew
are very young men before you --

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.397
When you met...
-Before.

00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:19.799
-Before...
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:20.866
You know how time works,
right?

00:03:20.866 --> 00:03:22.668
I'm not breaking
any news here, but...

00:03:22.668 --> 00:03:23.902
-I was going to say,
"That's so sweet."

00:03:23.902 --> 00:03:26.606
Oh, he means 10 years ago.
Got it. Yeah.

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:28.007
-You met
when you were very young.

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:30.042
And now, obviously, you have
these very accomplished careers,

00:03:30.042 --> 00:03:33.246
and it does seem
certainly from the outside

00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:35.181
that you genuinely
like one another.

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:36.548
-We love each other.

00:03:36.548 --> 00:03:39.018
It took me a decade
to get back to Broadway.

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:40.819
I, since "Book of Mormon,"

00:03:40.819 --> 00:03:43.556
I've been looking for something
to get back with,

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:45.724
and I got sent this script
by Alex Timbers,

00:03:45.724 --> 00:03:47.160
a brilliant director.
-Yes.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.427
And who also did
"Here Lies Love."

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:49.762
-Yeah.
-Which is phenomenal.

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:50.763
Everybody should go see
that show.

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:52.732
It's phenomenal.

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:56.368
And I love that they applauded
more for Andrew,

00:03:56.368 --> 00:03:57.637
who's not here than you,

00:03:57.637 --> 00:03:59.305
who is here and giving them
entertainment right now.

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:01.140
-Andrew is such a...
-Shame on all of you.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.308
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.276
But there -- there's
there's just something

00:04:04.276 --> 00:04:06.078
about finding the right thing.

00:04:06.078 --> 00:04:07.813
And Timbers called us up
and he said,

00:04:07.813 --> 00:04:09.348
"I think you guys
should read this.

00:04:09.348 --> 00:04:11.450
I directed it
Off-Broadway years ago.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.018
It's perfect
for the two of you."

00:04:13.018 --> 00:04:14.386
We read it and we fell
in love with it.

00:04:14.386 --> 00:04:16.555
It was exactly comedically
what I was looking for.

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:19.192
-It's so great.
And now you -- your kids,

00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:22.194
I'm curious about your kids,
9 and 12, two girls.

00:04:22.194 --> 00:04:25.265
I assume they've been to many
Broadway shows at this point.

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:26.865
-That assumption is incorrect.
-Wow.

00:04:26.865 --> 00:04:28.667
-They've been they've been
to many shows.

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:30.603
Not -- not -- Well, I guess
on tour they've been.

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:31.771
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:32.805
Their favorite show
is "Matilda the Musical."

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:34.006
-A great show.

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:35.675
-They've seen that like
six times in London.

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:39.212
And I took them
to a bunch of theater,

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:42.081
but they came to see me...
-In this?

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:44.784
-...in this, which was a very
unique experience.

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:46.986
They've seen me in things like
"Frozen" and things like that.

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:49.989
They've never seen me
on stage before.

00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:54.860
And so the girls came, and they
came to my opening and my 9,

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:58.464
my 9-year-old kept waving
to me the entire show,

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:00.199
and I was like, "Stop it,
you're" --

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:01.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:03.870
"You're embarrassing daddy."

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:07.440
But my my older one, Ava,
was concerned

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.744
because I got one of these
infamous sinus infections

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:13.146
last week on opening night.

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:15.848
So my voice started to go in

00:05:15.848 --> 00:05:17.483
in the at the beginning
of the show,

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:19.485
and Ava started crying
because she could tell

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:22.354
that my voice started going
and she looked at her mom

00:05:22.354 --> 00:05:24.123
and she's like, "Is,
"Dada going to be alright?

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:26.893
Is Dada going to be?"
And backstage that night,

00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:29.594
my singing teacher,
Liz Caplan, was in the audience.

00:05:29.594 --> 00:05:31.597
So she comes backstage

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:34.967
and she literally does like
a Mr. Miyagi from "Karate Kid"

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:37.736
and starts getting in there,
and I could suddenly sing again.

00:05:37.736 --> 00:05:41.674
And my daughter looked like
Daniel Larusso's girlfriend

00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:43.776
at the end of "Karate Kid."
She's like, "Yeah!"

00:05:43.776 --> 00:05:45.178
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:47.980
She was so proud,
so happy.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:51.283
-I will say, when --
when, you know,

00:05:51.283 --> 00:05:53.286
I've had family
come to shows before,

00:05:53.286 --> 00:05:56.022
and my absolute nightmare
would be

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:57.423
if I could see two of them,

00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:59.725
and one of them was waving
and the other one was crying.

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:01.760
-Yes.
-I mean, that is like...

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.663
When people like my wife
is always like, "Don't over --

00:06:03.663 --> 00:06:05.731
It's fine. A lot of friends
are going to come."

00:06:05.731 --> 00:06:07.166
I'm like, "But what
if I make eye contact?"

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:09.702
That would just be like one --
Like, if my mom was waving.

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:13.639
-It's too much pressure
to have both waving and crying

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:15.607
and an audience
just wanting to laugh.

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:17.577
-You play two people
with the dream

00:06:17.577 --> 00:06:18.844
of getting a Broadway show.

00:06:18.844 --> 00:06:21.647
So you're basically looking
for a producer that's --

00:06:21.647 --> 00:06:23.115
And you're sort
of pitching this idea.

00:06:23.115 --> 00:06:24.616
-Yeah.
So the idea of the show

00:06:24.616 --> 00:06:27.286
is that these guys have written
a show about Johann Gutenberg,

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:28.788
the inventor
of the printing press.

00:06:28.788 --> 00:06:32.057
I like to call this show the
"Hamilton" of our generation.

00:06:32.057 --> 00:06:35.261
And they -- Even though,
technically, "Hamilton"

00:06:35.261 --> 00:06:37.563
is the "Hamilton"
of its own generation.

00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:40.432
So we, you know,
we play 20 characters each,

00:06:40.432 --> 00:06:44.503
Andrew and I,
we wear 150 hats to distinguish

00:06:44.503 --> 00:06:45.871
between each of these
characters,

00:06:45.871 --> 00:06:48.574
which is a nightmare for someone
as uncoordinated as I am.

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:50.475
And -- And the two of us
are on stage

00:06:50.475 --> 00:06:52.444
throughout the entire thing.
-Singing, dancing.

00:06:52.444 --> 00:06:53.745
-Singing, dancing.
-Do you collapse when it's over?

00:06:53.745 --> 00:06:58.217
-Oh, my God,
I literally on two show days

00:06:58.217 --> 00:06:59.985
I will go backstage and sleep

00:06:59.985 --> 00:07:02.454
on what Andrew calls
my FEMA cot.

00:07:02.454 --> 00:07:06.492
I have this little cot
that looks really upsetting

00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:09.295
and -- and it doesn't
quite fit my body on it,

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:12.231
so I just sort of like
hang over the edge of it.

00:07:12.231 --> 00:07:15.134
But I sleep on my FEMA cot,
I get a little nap in,

00:07:15.134 --> 00:07:17.803
I have a nice little dinner,
and then I get back to it.

00:07:17.803 --> 00:07:20.907
But, you know, those two show
days, they're, it's a lot.

00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:22.074
-You you mentioned "Frozen."

00:07:22.074 --> 00:07:23.476
I told my kids
you were coming on tonight.

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:24.777
-Aww.
-They were very excited.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:27.579
But then it always --
This moment of sadness hits

00:07:27.579 --> 00:07:29.948
when they realize it's not
the actual animated character.

00:07:29.948 --> 00:07:31.317
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:07:31.317 --> 00:07:33.219
-And at that moment
of sadness hits my daughters

00:07:33.219 --> 00:07:34.520
every day when I come home.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:35.521
[ Laughter ]

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:36.655
Where they go,

00:07:36.655 --> 00:07:37.956
this isn't what we
signed up for.

00:07:37.956 --> 00:07:39.658
-But at least on sunny days,
they don't have to worry.

00:07:39.658 --> 00:07:40.926
-[ Laughs ]
-That's the good news.

00:07:40.926 --> 00:07:42.694
Hey, what a delight.
We got to do it again soon.

00:07:42.694 --> 00:07:43.695
-I love you asked.
-It was so great to have you

00:07:43.695 --> 00:07:44.797
back in New York City?

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:46.798
-I love you guys.
Josh Gad.

00:07:46.798 --> 00:07:48.133
"Gutenberg! The Musical!,"

00:07:48.133 --> 00:07:49.869
now playing
at the James Earl Jones Theatre.

00:07:49.869 --> 00:07:50.937
Go see it and stick around.

00:07:50.937 --> 00:07:52.238
We'll be right back
with Joe Paris.

